3	scans/2026/09/math-problem_4R3LV6Y.jpg	ready	Question 2\n\nGiven the function\n\nf(x) = e^(x² - x)\n\nfind:\na) the intervals of increase and decrease\nb) the value(s) of x where f has a local extremum\nc) the intervals of concavity and any inflection point(s)	\N	Calculus	Given the function f(x) = e^(x² - x), find:\na) the intervals of increase and decrease\nb) the value(s) of x where f has a local extremum\nc) the intervals of concavity and any inflection point(s)	Step 1: Find the first derivative, f'(x)\nUsing the chain rule for f(x) = e^(x² - x):\nf'(x) = d/dx(x² - x) × e^(x² - x)\nf'(x) = (2x - 1)e^(x² - x) [M1]\nStep 2: Find critical points and intervals of increase and decrease (Part a)\nSet f'(x) = 0 to find critical points:\n(2x - 1)e^(x² - x) = 0\nSince e^(x² - x) > 0 for all real x, 2x - 1 = 0, giving x = 1/2. [M1]\n\nTesting the sign of f'(x):\n- For x < 1/2: (2x - 1) < 0, so f'(x) < 0 (function is decreasing).\n- For x > 1/2: (2x - 1) > 0, so f'(x) > 0 (function is increasing).\n\nIntervals:\n- Decreasing on: (-∞, 1/2) or x < 1/2 [A1]\n- Increasing on: (1/2, ∞) or x > 1/2 [A1]\nStep 3: Determine local extrema (Part b)\nAt x = 1/2, f'(x) changes sign from negative to positive.\nBy the First Derivative Test, f has a local minimum at x = 1/2. [M1]\n\nf(1/2) = e^((1/2)² - 1/2) = e^(1/4 - 1/2) = e^(-1/4)\nThus, a local minimum occurs at x = 1/2, with value e^(-1/4). [A1]\nStep 4: Find the second derivative, f''(x) (Part c)\nDifferentiate f'(x) = (2x - 1)e^(x² - x) using the product rule:\nf''(x) = d/dx(2x - 1) × e^(x² - x) + (2x - 1) × d/dx[e^(x² - x)] [M1]\nf''(x) = 2e^(x² - x) + (2x - 1)(2x - 1)e^(x² - x)\nf''(x) = [2 + (2x - 1)²] e^(x² - x)\nf''(x) = [2 + 4x² - 4x + 1] e^(x² - x)\nf''(x) = (4x² - 4x + 3)e^(x² - x) [A1]\nStep 5: Determine concavity and inflection points\nTo find concavity, analyze the sign of f''(x):\nNotice that 4x² - 4x + 3 = 4(x - 1/2)² + 2, which is strictly greater than 0 for all x ∈ ℝ.\n(Alternatively, the discriminant Δ = (-4)² - 4(4)(3) = 16 - 48 = -32 < 0, meaning 4x² - 4x + 3 has no real roots and is strictly positive).\nSince e^(x² - x) > 0 and (4x² - 4x + 3) > 0 for all x, f''(x) > 0 for all x ∈ ℝ. [M1]\n\nConclusion:\n- Concave upwards on: (-∞, ∞) or for all x ∈ ℝ [A1]\n- Inflection points: None, since f''(x) is never zero and never changes sign. [B1]\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n1. Forgetting to apply the product rule when finding the second derivative of (2x - 1)e^(x² - x).\n2. Incorrectly attempting to solve 4x² - 4x + 3 = 0 using the quadratic formula without checking the discriminant first, leading to non-existent real inflection points.\n3. Omitting the fact that e^(x² - x) is strictly positive for all x, which simplifies sign testing.	[{"mark": null, "step": 1, "text": "Step 1: Find the first derivative, f'(x)\\nUsing the chain rule for f(x) = e^(x² - x):\\nf'(x) = d/dx(x² - x) × e^(x² - x)\\nf'(x) = (2x - 1)e^(x² - x) [M1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 2, "text": "Step 2: Find critical points and intervals of increase and decrease (Part a)\\nSet f'(x) = 0 to find critical points:\\n(2x - 1)e^(x² - x) = 0\\nSince e^(x² - x) > 0 for all real x, 2x - 1 = 0, giving x = 1/2. [M1]\\n\\nTesting the sign of f'(x):\\n- For x < 1/2: (2x - 1) < 0, so f'(x) < 0 (function is decreasing).\\n- For x > 1/2: (2x - 1) > 0, so f'(x) > 0 (function is increasing).\\n\\nIntervals:\\n- Decreasing on: (-∞, 1/2) or x < 1/2 [A1]\\n- Increasing on: (1/2, ∞) or x > 1/2 [A1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 3, "text": "Step 3: Determine local extrema (Part b)\\nAt x = 1/2, f'(x) changes sign from negative to positive.\\nBy the First Derivative Test, f has a local minimum at x = 1/2. [M1]\\n\\nf(1/2) = e^((1/2)² - 1/2) = e^(1/4 - 1/2) = e^(-1/4)\\nThus, a local minimum occurs at x = 1/2, with value e^(-1/4). [A1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 4, "text": "Step 4: Find the second derivative, f''(x) (Part c)\\nDifferentiate f'(x) = (2x - 1)e^(x² - x) using the product rule:\\nf''(x) = d/dx(2x - 1) × e^(x² - x) + (2x - 1) × d/dx[e^(x² - x)] [M1]\\nf''(x) = 2e^(x² - x) + (2x - 1)(2x - 1)e^(x² - x)\\nf''(x) = [2 + (2x - 1)²] e^(x² - x)\\nf''(x) = [2 + 4x² - 4x + 1] e^(x² - x)\\nf''(x) = (4x² - 4x + 3)e^(x² - x) [A1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 5, "text": "Step 5: Determine concavity and inflection points\\nTo find concavity, analyze the sign of f''(x):\\nNotice that 4x² - 4x + 3 = 4(x - 1/2)² + 2, which is strictly greater than 0 for all x ∈ ℝ.\\n(Alternatively, the discriminant Δ = (-4)² - 4(4)(3) = 16 - 48 = -32 < 0, meaning 4x² - 4x + 3 has no real roots and is strictly positive).\\nSince e^(x² - x) > 0 and (4x² - 4x + 3) > 0 for all x, f''(x) > 0 for all x ∈ ℝ. [M1]\\n\\nConclusion:\\n- Concave upwards on: (-∞, ∞) or for all x ∈ ℝ [A1]\\n- Inflection points: None, since f''(x) is never zero and never changes sign. [B1]\\n\\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\\n1. Forgetting to apply the product rule when finding the second derivative of (2x - 1)e^(x² - x).\\n2. Incorrectly attempting to solve 4x² - 4x + 3 = 0 using the quadratic formula without checking the discriminant first, leading to non-existent real inflection points.\\n3. Omitting the fact that e^(x² - x) is strictly positive for all x, which simplifies sign testing."}]	a) Decreasing on (-∞, 1/2) and Increasing on (1/2, ∞)\nb) Local minimum at x = 1/2 (value: e^(-1/4))\nc) Concave upwards on (-∞, ∞); No inflection points	[]		2026-09-13 15:57:15.638846+00	2026-09-13 16:00:18.664686+00	1
4	scans/2026/09/math-problem_tx0PBTZ.jpg	ready	{\n  "text": "Determine whether the function\\n\\n$$f(x) = \\\\begin{cases} 2x^2, & x \\\\le			Determine whether the function $$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2x^2, & x \\le 1 \\\\ 3x - 1, & x > 1 \\end{cases}$$ is continuous at $x = 1$.	**Step 1: Understand the conditions for continuity at a point**\nFor a function $f(x)$ to be continuous at a point $x = a$, three conditions must be satisfied:\n1. $f(a)$ must be defined.\n2. The limit $\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x)$ must exist, which means the Left-Hand Limit (LHL) equal to the Right-Hand Limit (RHL):\n$$\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x)$$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) = f(a)$.\n**Step 2: Evaluate $f(1)$**\nTo find $f(1)$, use the branch corresponding to $x \\le 1$, which is $f(x) = 2x^2$.\n$$f(1) = 2(1)^2 = 2$$ [B1]\n**Step 3: Evaluate the Left-Hand Limit (LHL)**\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left ($x < 1$), use $f(x) = 2x^2$.\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^-} (2x^2) = 2(1)^2 = 2$$ [M1]\n**Step 4: Evaluate the Right-Hand Limit (RHL)**\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the right ($x > 1$), use $f(x) = 3x - 1$.\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^+} (3x - 1) = 3(1) - 1 = 2$$ [M1]\n**Step 5: Compare the limits and conclude**\nSince $\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = 2$, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2$.\n\nFurthermore, $\\lim_{x \\to 1} f(x) = f(1) = 2$. [A1]\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$. [A1]\n**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\n1. *Evaluating only one-sided limit*: Students often only test $x > 1$ or $x \\le 1$ instead of showing both the Left-Hand Limit (LHL) and Right-Hand Limit (RHL).\n2. *Using the wrong branch for $f(a)$*: Students sometimes substitute $x = 1$ into $3x - 1$ instead of $2x^2$, ignoring the inequality signs.\n3. *Confusing continuity with differentiability*: Showing that the limit exists is not enough; one must explicitly state and show that $\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) = f(a)$.	[{"mark": null, "step": 1, "text": "**Step 1: Understand the conditions for continuity at a point**\\nFor a function $f(x)$ to be continuous at a point $x = a$, three conditions must be satisfied:\\n1. $f(a)$ must be defined.\\n2. The limit $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to a} f(x)$ must exist, which means the Left-Hand Limit (LHL) equal to the Right-Hand Limit (RHL):\\n$$\\\\lim_{x \\\\to a^-} f(x) = \\\\lim_{x \\\\to a^+} f(x)$$\\n3. $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to a} f(x) = f(a)$."}, {"mark": null, "step": 2, "text": "**Step 2: Evaluate $f(1)$**\\nTo find $f(1)$, use the branch corresponding to $x \\\\le 1$, which is $f(x) = 2x^2$.\\n$$f(1) = 2(1)^2 = 2$$ [B1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 3, "text": "**Step 3: Evaluate the Left-Hand Limit (LHL)**\\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left ($x < 1$), use $f(x) = 2x^2$.\\n$$\\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^-} (2x^2) = 2(1)^2 = 2$$ [M1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 4, "text": "**Step 4: Evaluate the Right-Hand Limit (RHL)**\\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the right ($x > 1$), use $f(x) = 3x - 1$.\\n$$\\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^+} (3x - 1) = 3(1) - 1 = 2$$ [M1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 5, "text": "**Step 5: Compare the limits and conclude**\\nSince $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1^+} f(x) = 2$, the overall limit exists and $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1} f(x) = 2$.\\n\\nFurthermore, $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to 1} f(x) = f(1) = 2$. [A1]\\n\\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$. [A1]"}, {"mark": null, "step": 6, "text": "**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\\n1. *Evaluating only one-sided limit*: Students often only test $x > 1$ or $x \\\\le 1$ instead of showing both the Left-Hand Limit (LHL) and Right-Hand Limit (RHL).\\n2. *Using the wrong branch for $f(a)$*: Students sometimes substitute $x = 1$ into $3x - 1$ instead of $2x^2$, ignoring the inequality signs.\\n3. *Confusing continuity with differentiability*: Showing that the limit exists is not enough; one must explicitly state and show that $\\\\lim_{x \\\\to a} f(x) = f(a)$."}]	The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$ because $\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = f(1) = 2$.	[]		2026-09-13 22:14:29.243078+00	2026-09-13 22:15:12.683869+00	1
